2026-11-22 09:22:30 - Agent_20261122092146 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed the three os.open call sites reported by py/dump-os-open (CWE-59 symlink following / truncation).

Findings:
1. src/filelock/_soft.py:27 — os.open with O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_EXCL|O_TRUNC. The O_EXCL flag guarantees the file is created atomically and never follows a pre-existing symlink (EEXIST is raised instead, and is caught/handled as "lock already exists"). No symlink-following risk. Classified false positive.
2. src/filelock/_windows.py:22 (os.open at line 28) — Windows-only path (msvcrt), uses O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC without O_NOFOLLOW. CWE-59 technically applies but Windows os.open/Python does not honor O_NOFOLLOW semantics the same way, the lock file path is supplied by the application developer, and the pre-existing symlink scenario requires local write access to the lock directory (same privilege level as the attack target). Not exploitable in a meaningful way — classified false positive, though hardening with O_NOINHERIT/reparse-point checks could be considered.
3. src/filelock/_unix.py:41-44 — UNIX path where the existence check (Path(...).exists()) is performed BEFORE os.open and O_CREAT is only added when the file doesn't exist. This is a classic TOCTOU: between the exists() check and os.open(), an attacker with write access to the lock directory can swap in a symlink to an arbitrary file. Combined with O_TRUNC|O_RDWR (no O_NOFOLLOW), os.open will follow the symlink and truncate the target file. Classified manual review / true-positive-adjacent — the filelock library explicitly documents lock file paths as trusted application configuration, so severity is low, but the missing O_NOFOLLOW + TOCTOU pattern is real. Also note that O_TRUNC is applied even when the file exists, meaning any symlink target gets truncated to zero bytes.

No additional independent vulnerabilities were discovered during the review.
