2026-11-22 09:22:57 - Agent_20261122092146 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed all three chmod/fchmod-related CodeQL results, which all point to the same location: src/filelock/_unix.py:46 (`os.fchmod(fd, self._context.mode)` inside `UnixFileLock._acquire`). Verified the mode originates from a user-configurable `mode` parameter (default 0o644) defined in `src/filelock/_api.py` (`BaseFileLock.__init__` / `FileLockMeta.__call__`), so library consumers control the permission bits and the default 0o644 for a lock file is standard, non-sensitive (lock files contain no data; `_unix.py`/`_soft.py` never write content, and soft lock unlinks the file). The `fchmod` call is wrapped in `suppress(PermissionError)`, and the initial creation uses `os.open(..., self._context.mode)` which is subject to umask. The related sinks in `src/filelock/_soft.py:27` and `src/filelock/_windows.py:28` use the same caller-controlled mode. No attacker-controlled path or broadening of permissions on files containing sensitive data was identified, so these `py/dump-chmod*` informational results are false positives for CWE-732.
